/-
OEIS A051451 (a(n) = lcm(1..x), x the n-th prime power).  Conjecture
(M. Winkler, 2013, as on the OEIS wiki conjecture list): for every n > 2 there
is a twin prime pair [p, p+2] with p < a(n) such that the *next* twin prime
pair above a(n) is [a(n)+p, a(n)+p+2].  False at n = 35.
(The current OEIS wording drops "next"; that version is not addressed.)

Let L = a(35) = lcm(1..89).  The first twin prime pair above L is
[L + 17159, L + 17161], and 17161 = 131^2 is not prime, so the offset is
not the lesser member of a twin prime pair.

Checked here (core Lean, `decide`):
* `L_val`: L = lcm(1..89) (as the product of the largest prime powers ≤ 89),
  and every prime q ≤ 89 divides L.
* `window`: for every m with 1 ≤ m < 17159, L + m or L + m + 2 has a
  divisor d with 1 < d < (that number): d = 2 for even m, a prime q ≤ 89
  dividing m or m + 2 (hence L + m or L + m + 2), or one of 309 explicit
  larger divisors (found with PARI).  So no twin pair has offset < 17159.
* `sq`: 17161 = 131 * 131.
Not checked here: that L + 17159 and L + 17161 are prime (PARI isprime,
APR-CL proof); this fixes the first twin pair at offset exactly 17159.
-/

